  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 958 AM MDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nSOME ISOLATED STORM CHANCES REMAIN FOR TODAY:\n\nThe dominant flow aloft will be mainly southwesterly around the \nwestern edge of the high pressure system to our east through at \nleast the weekend. This, in turn, will keep moisture advection \naround. Hence, precipitation chances remain in the forecast for the \nforeseeable future, particularly for the higher terrain.\n\nThe last few days have seen appreciable moisture for this region of \nthe country, but storms have been short lived due to a lack of shear \nand dense cloud cover filling in during the afternoons. This could \nvery well be the case today as well. Current satellite imagery this \nmorning depicts denser cloud cover over the San Juans and parts of \nsoutheast Utah, with clearing everywhere else. This will help paint \na better picture for storm chances this afternoon, with higher storm \nchances occurring where daytime heating was allowed to materialize. \nThere is also a possibility for marginally increased shear this \nafternoon as a mild jet streak traverses through the southwesterly \nflow. Although, ensembles show falling PWATs for much of the region \nbeginning today and lasting through the weekend.\n\nAll that being said, most stronger storms capable of small hail and \nlocalized heavy rainfall (or the flash flooding concerns that come \nwith it) are expected to stay confined to the high terrain today. As \npreviously mentioned, if daytime heating dominates in the lower \nelevation areas, that may be enough for storms to initialize over \nthose areas and cannot be ruled out completely at this point. All \nvulnerable areas (including burn scars) across the entire CWA will \nstill be at risk of flash flooding or debris flows today in spite of \nthe more localized nature of storm coverage. \n\nWEEKEND + BEYOND OUTLOOK: \n\nEven as moisture is on the steady decline, southwesterly flow in \ncombination with moisture advection will be enough to support daily \nprecipitation chances around the mountains this weekend. Storm \nimpacts appear to be on the milder side as upper level support seems \nweak. Although, keep checking the forecast as changes are possible \nwhen it comes to these patterns. \n\nAs we move into next week, more uncertainties exist regarding \nprecipitation chances as models begin to diverge from each other. \nOtherwise, look for temperatures to gradually increase each day into \nnext week.",
